### Ali Syed Ayan: job posting, keywords, and bullet selection

When the user supplies a **job posting URL** (or full pasted JD) for Ali’s tailored `<PersonSlug>_<CompanySlug>_<RoleSlug>_resume.tex`:

1. **Retrieve the posting text** (full description when accessible; otherwise the visible summary plus user paste). Extract about **30 keywords or phrases** (skills, tools, responsibilities, domain terms).
2. **Map to the master:** In `people/Ali-Syed-Ayan/complete/master-resume.tex` and `complete/resume-knowledge-base.yaml`, find (a) **work bullets** that match each keyword or close synonyms, (b) **Projects** whose stack or outcome matches posting terms, (c) **Skills** lines or tokens that **exactly or closely** match those keywords.
3. **Build the tailored resume** by **including** those bullets, projects, and skill tokens (still **no invented employers, dates, or metrics**). Prefer **coverage of the posting** over arbitrary caps **while the PDF remains exactly one page**.
4. **Page fill:** The tailored PDF should **read as a full page**, not a short column with a large empty block at the bottom. If `pdflatex` leaves **noticeable whitespace** on page 1, add more master-backed, posting-relevant lines (work bullets, projects, or skills) until the layout looks balanced. Do **not** cheat with extra vertical glue or sub-template margins.
5. **Bullet count caps (3 for the two most recent roles, ≥2 for others)** apply to **Zhao Yanbo** and to **Ali only as a pagination fallback:** use them **after** `pdflatex` shows **more than one page**, when trimming alongside **2–3** one-liners and **weakest-bullet → one-liner** swaps per the workflow below. Until length forces trims, Ali’s **default priority** is **keyword and JD alignment**, not fixed bullet counts. If LinkedIn hides text behind **Show more**, expand it or ask the user to paste the full description before finalizing keywords.

**Unchanged for Ali:** STAR shape, **two-line bullets must either fill both wrapped lines with substance or collapse to a single one-liner** (no half-empty second line), punctuation (no em dash, light parentheses, no `~` / `$\sim$` for “about”), hyperlink rules, and the **pagination** sequence (**2–3** one-liners, then **weakest-bullet → one-liner** swap; optional oldest-role omission only with user confirmation).

